<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mtskheta</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mtskheta is a small town north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, where the Aragvi meets the Kura. It was the capital of the ancient kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, or Kartli, until about the late 5th or 6th century, and the centre of its conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svetitskhoveli-cathedral">Svetitskhoveli Cathedral</a> and the Jvari monastery on the hill above are among the most important churches of Georgia and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mtskheta.jpg" alt="Mtskheta" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jelger Groeneveld · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_to_Mtskheta_from_Jvari.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mtskheta lies on a lowland surrounded by mountains, at the confluence of the Kura, called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kura-river">Mtkvari</a> in Georgian, and the Aragvi. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-caucasus">Greater Caucasus</a> rise to the north and the Lesser Caucasus to the south. Archaeology traces settlement in the area from the 2nd millennium BCE to the early 1st millennium CE, and numerous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> burials show that it was already a significant settlement at the start of the 1st millennium BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartlis-tskhovreba">The Georgian Chronicles</a> say that Mtskheta was founded by Mtskhetos, son of Kartlos, the eponymous ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a>. Historians more readily accept another account, that the Meschian tribes founded it in the 5th century BCE. It was the capital of the early Georgian Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> from the 3rd century BCE. The chronicles have <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> conquering it, which some historians do not accept. The first Georgian king, Parnavaz, built a new wall around it at the start of the 3rd century BCE, and his son Saurmag strengthened it. The city was strongly fortified, with the citadel of Armazi on Mount Bagineti and forts at Tsitsamuri and Sarkine.</p><p>Mtskheta was a site of early Christian activity, and in 337 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> was proclaimed the state religion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>. A small wooden church built in the centre of the city soon after the conversion became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svetitskhoveli-cathedral">Svetitskhoveli Cathedral</a>, and in the 5th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakhtang-i-of-iberia">Vakhtang Gorgasali</a> built a large basilica on the site. The first katolikos was elected in the 5th century and resided in Mtskheta. Vakhtang's successor, King Dachi, moved the capital to the more defensible <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> in the early 6th century, following his father's will. Mtskheta nevertheless remained the coronation and burial place of most Georgian kings until the end of Georgian political independence in the 19th century.</p><p>The city was heavily damaged during the Umayyad defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> between 736 and 739. It regained its standing under King George I and Katolikos Melchizedek I, who oversaw the building of the present Svetitskhoveli and Samtavro cathedrals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> burned the city in the 15th century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> rebuilt Svetitskhoveli in 1425. Further attacks, including one in 1625, reduced it so that by 1801, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, it was little more than a village.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mtskheta remains the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgian-orthodox-church">Georgian Orthodox Church</a>. Svetitskhoveli Cathedral, the Samtavro Monastery and the Jvari Monastery are among the most significant monuments of Georgian Christian architecture, and their early inscriptions are a valuable reference for the origins of the Georgian alphabet. The Historical Monuments of Mtskheta became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1994. Nearby lie the ruins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armaztsikhe">Armaztsikhe</a> fortress.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mtskheta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The rock-cut monastery of Vardzia, consecrated in the 1180s, the Bagrati Cathedral at Kutaisi and the rebuilt Svetitskhoveli Cathedral at <b>Mtskheta</b>, finished around 1029, show the ambition of Georgian church architecture, while goldsmiths and fresco painters produced works of high quality.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>These accounts were written many centuries later and mix history with legend, but archaeology confirms that a centralised kingdom with a fortified royal seat at <b>Mtskheta</b> and the acropolis of Armaztsikhe was taking shape in the early Hellenistic period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kingdom of Iberia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ksani valley north of <b>Mtskheta</b> was organised as a saeristavo, or duchy, of the Kingdom of Georgia, and from the late 14th century the office became hereditary in a local family known as the Ksani eristavis.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-ksani">Duchy of Ksani</a></li><li><blockquote>Stephen I built the Jvari church above <b>Mtskheta</b>, a model for Georgian architecture.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-iberia">Principality of Iberia</a></li></ul></section>
